    $astroGas_master$> The terrestrial worlds could be reasoned about as *surfaces* — craters, volcanoes, canyons, dust. The objects in this chapter have no surfaces at all. There is no ground on Jupiter, no place to stand, no boundary between air and rock, no line you cross where you could say *here the planet begins.* What there is instead is a continuous, thousandfold, ten-thousandfold thickening of the same substance, from a wisp of hydrogen at the top to a metal at the bottom, with every intermediate state in between and no phase boundary you could point at along the way. That absence is the intellectual centre of everything that follows. Almost every strange fact about Jupiter and Saturn — the banding, the magnetic field, the internal heat, the fact that Saturn would float in a bathtub — follows from the single circumstance that these worlds are made almost entirely of the lightest, simplest, most compressible element there is, in quantities large enough that the element stops behaving like itself.

## The payoff of the snow line

The framework built for the terrestrial planets did real work: **size and distance, plus the contingency of water**, explain most of what separates Mercury from Earth from Mars. Small worlds cool fast, lose their dynamos, and lose their air. Distance sets the temperature, and temperature decides whether water is vapour, liquid, or ice.

The giant planets are on the far side of a line drawn much earlier: **the snow line.** Beyond about 2.7 AU, water ice condenses, the surface density of solid material jumps by a factor of two to four, cores grow fast enough to reach the critical ~10 Earth masses before the gas disk evaporates, and runaway accretion begins. Everything inside that line got scraps of rock. Everything outside it got the disk itself. Jupiter and Saturn are, in a sense, the payoff of the snow line — and also something harder to picture, because they have no ground.

{{image: Jupiter | Jupiter photographed by the Hubble Space Telescope. Everything visible here — the belts, the zones, the Great Red Spot — is a skin about four parts in a thousand deep, painted onto an object whose interior we can only infer from gravity, magnetism, and heat.}}

## Fifty-eight minutes

On 7 December 1995, a 339-kilogram titanium-and-carbon cone struck the top of Jupiter's atmosphere at **47.4 kilometres per second.**

It is still the fastest atmospheric entry any human artefact has ever attempted. The **Galileo probe** had been released from its mother ship five months earlier on a ballistic trajectory with no ability to steer, no ability to abort, and no ability to report until it was already inside. It hit the atmosphere at an angle chosen to a fraction of a degree: too shallow and it would skip off into space; too steep and it would be destroyed before it could slow down.

Deceleration peaked at roughly **228 g.** The heat shield — 152 kg of carbon phenolic, nearly half the probe's entry mass — ablated away, and the ablation pattern was itself so far from prediction that it became a research result. Then the parachute opened, and for **fifty-eight minutes** an object built by human beings fell through the atmosphere of Jupiter and told us what was there.

What it found was almost entirely wrong.

The winds did not die away with depth as expected; they **increased**, reaching some 700 km/h and showing no sign of stopping, which meant the weather was not being driven from above by sunlight but from below by internal heat. The lightning was rarer than predicted. The atmosphere was hotter and denser than models said. And most consequentially, the water — the water that theories of Jupiter's formation absolutely required — was almost **absent.** Oxygen came back depleted by a factor of several relative to solar.

For years this was a genuine crisis. And then it was resolved in the most humbling possible way: the probe had fallen, by bad luck, into a **5-micron hot spot** — one of a small number of unusually dry, cloud-free downdrafts that cover a tiny fraction of the planet. It had parachuted into Jupiter's equivalent of the Sahara and reported that Earth has no oceans.

Hold onto that story. It is not the first time a single, technically flawless measurement has misled an entire field because the *sampling* was unrepresentative — the Viking Labeled Release experiment on Mars was another. **A perfect instrument in the wrong place is not data; it is an anecdote with error bars.**

The probe stopped transmitting at **22 bar**, about 153 km below the 1-bar level, at a temperature around 153 °C. It was not crushed. It was not destroyed. Its transmitter simply cooked, and the probe kept falling — through pressures of hundreds, then thousands, then millions of bars, through hydrogen that gradually stopped being a gas and started being a liquid and then a metal, and it is still down there, dissolved into a planet that has no bottom. That is the object we are going to try to understand.

## The scale problem

Start with a number that reframes the solar system.

| Body | Mass (Earth = 1) |
|---|---|
| **Jupiter** | **317.8** |
| Saturn | 95.2 |
| Neptune | 17.1 |
| Uranus | 14.5 |
| Earth | 1.00 |
| Venus | 0.815 |
| Mars | 0.107 |
| Mercury | 0.055 |

Add up **every planet except Jupiter** — Saturn, both ice giants, all four terrestrials — and you get 128.8 Earth masses. Jupiter alone is **2.47 times** that total.

Put Jupiter and Saturn together and they are **92.5% of all planetary mass in the solar system.** Every other planet, every moon, every asteroid, every comet, the entire Kuiper belt — all of it is a rounding error, a **7.5%** garnish on a system that consists, to first approximation, of a star and two balls of hydrogen.

This is not a curiosity. It is the correct mental picture, and it is worth deliberately installing over the schoolroom one in which eight planets sit in a row like beads. **The solar system is the Sun, then Jupiter, then a long tail of debris — and we live in the debris.**

### Why is Jupiter not a star?

The obvious next question. Jupiter is made of the same stuff as the Sun — hydrogen and helium in roughly solar proportions — and it is enormous. Why did it not ignite? Because it is nowhere near big enough, and the gap is not close.

- Jupiter is **1/1048 of a solar mass.**
- **Deuterium fusion** — the easiest nuclear reaction available, which is what defines the lower edge of the brown dwarfs — begins at about **13 Jupiter masses.** Jupiter is short by a factor of 13.
- **Hydrogen fusion** — being a star, properly — requires about **0.075–0.080 M☉**, roughly **80 Jupiter masses.** Jupiter is short by a factor of **80.**

So the popular line that Jupiter is a "failed star" is misleading in a specific and instructive way. Jupiter did not fail at anything. It is not a star that fell short; it is **a planet that succeeded**, formed by an entirely different mechanism — core accretion, from the inside out, starting with ice and rock — rather than by the top-down gravitational collapse of a cloud fragment that makes stars. The two objects share a composition and almost nothing else about their history.

### The strangest consequence: bigger is not bigger

Here is a fact that ought to feel wrong. If you took Jupiter and added mass to it — doubled it, tripled it, made it ten times heavier — it would **not get much bigger.** Past about 3–4 Jupiter masses it would start getting **smaller.**

The reason is that hydrogen at these pressures is held up not by thermal pressure but increasingly by **electron degeneracy pressure** — the quantum-mechanical refusal of electrons to occupy the same state. For a degenerate object, radius *decreases* with mass ($R \propto M^{-1/3}$), the opposite of ordinary intuition. Jupiter sits close to the maximum radius a cold hydrogen object can have.

The observational payoff is immediate and, once you know it, unforgettable: **brown dwarfs — objects up to 80 times Jupiter's mass — are approximately Jupiter-sized.** Out in the exoplanet catalogues there are worlds with eight times Jupiter's mass and Jupiter's radius, sitting alongside worlds with Jupiter's mass and Jupiter's radius. Radius is nearly useless as a mass indicator in this regime. That single degeneracy relation, first written down for white dwarfs, is why.

## A world with no surface

Here is the claim made precise. When we quote "the radius of Jupiter" as 71,492 km, we are quoting a **convention**: the level at which the pressure equals **1 bar**, Earth's sea-level pressure. There is nothing physically special about that level. It is a bookkeeping decision, chosen so that planetary scientists can talk to each other. Choose 10 bar instead and Jupiter's radius changes by a few hundred kilometres.

Below that arbitrary line, here is what you would pass through on the way down.

**The weather layer (0 to a few hundred km).** Ammonia ice clouds at the top, then ammonium hydrosulfide, then — deeper, and only sometimes — water clouds. This is everything you have ever seen in a photograph of Jupiter. Size it honestly. The atmospheric **scale height** — the distance over which pressure falls by a factor of *e* — is

$$H = \frac{kT}{\mu m_H g} \approx 25 \ \text{km on Jupiter} \quad (51 \ \text{km on Saturn}, \ 7.4 \ \text{km on Earth})$$

so the entire visible cloud structure spans a few hundred kilometres at most: about **0.4% of the radius.** Every image of Jupiter you have ever seen shows you a skin four parts in a thousand deep, painted onto an object we cannot see.

**Molecular hydrogen (a few hundred km to ~14,000 km).** Pressure and temperature climb. The gas gets denser, and at some point — with no boundary, no surface, no splash — it becomes indistinguishable from a liquid. There is no ocean surface on Jupiter because above the critical point of hydrogen **there is no distinction between gas and liquid to have a surface between.** You would simply notice, if you were still alive, that the stuff around you had become thick.

**Metallic hydrogen (~14,000 km down to the core region).** At roughly **1–2 megabars** and several thousand kelvin, something remarkable happens: hydrogen molecules dissociate and then ionise, and the electrons are no longer bound to individual protons. They become free to move through the bulk — exactly as in a metal. **Liquid metallic hydrogen** is an excellent electrical conductor, and it makes up the great majority of Jupiter's volume and mass.

This is not a comfortable extrapolation. Metallic hydrogen was predicted by Wigner and Huntington in 1935, and its production in a terrestrial laboratory remains **contested to this day** — claims of static-compression metallic hydrogen have been made and disputed repeatedly, and dynamic-compression (shock) experiments on deuterium at Sandia and the National Ignition Facility have been essential in pinning down the equation of state. So there is a genuinely satisfying loop here: *we go to Jupiter partly to do condensed-matter physics we cannot do on Earth, and we do condensed-matter physics on Earth partly to understand Jupiter.*

**The centre.** Something like **40–45 megabars** and roughly **20,000 K** — considerably hotter than the Sun's visible surface (5,772 K), though of course not remotely hot enough to fuse anything.

### The bathtub fact, taken seriously

Jupiter's mean density is **1,326 kg/m³.** Saturn's is **687 kg/m³** — genuinely less than water. The undergraduate joke is that Saturn would float, and the joke is fine as far as it goes (you would need a bathtub 120,000 km across and Saturn would fall apart long before you filled it), but the *physics* underneath is the point.

Both planets are made of essentially the same material in roughly the same proportions. Jupiter is nearly **twice as dense** as Saturn. Why? Not composition — **self-compression.** Jupiter is 3.3 times more massive, so its interior squeezes itself far harder, and hydrogen is enormously compressible. The density difference between the two largest planets in the solar system is almost entirely a statement about how much a pile of hydrogen crushes itself.

### Spin, and the shape it produces

These planets rotate absurdly fast for their size. Jupiter turns in **9 h 55 m**, Saturn in about **10 h 33 m** — a day of under eleven hours on bodies eleven and nine times Earth's width. A point on Jupiter's equator is moving at **12.6 km/s**, forty-five thousand kilometres an hour. The result is visible in any decent telescope: they are **visibly not round.**

| | Oblateness $(R_{eq}-R_{pol})/R_{eq}$ |
|---|---|
| Earth | 0.0034 (1 part in 298) |
| Jupiter | **0.0649** (1 part in 15) |
| Saturn | **0.0980** (1 part in 10) |

Saturn is nearly **10% wider than it is tall.** And the reason Saturn is *more* squashed than Jupiter despite spinning slightly slower is a lovely piece of reasoning: the centrifugal acceleration at the equator, as a fraction of gravity, is **0.089 for Jupiter but 0.158 for Saturn** — because Saturn's surface gravity is less than half Jupiter's. Same spin, weaker grip, more bulge. That oblateness is not an aesthetic detail. It is a **measuring instrument**, and it is how we see inside.

## How to look inside a planet you cannot enter

We have no seismometers on Jupiter, no boreholes, no samples. And yet the interior structure of Jupiter is now known better than the interior of Mars. The tool is **gravity.**

A perfectly spherical, uniform planet has a gravitational field that falls off exactly as $1/r^2$. A real, spinning, oblate, centrally-concentrated planet does not. Its external field is written as a series:

$$V(r,\theta) = \frac{GM}{r}\left[1 - \sum_{n} J_{n}\left(\frac{R}{r}\right)^{n} P_{n}(\cos\theta)\right]$$

The coefficients $J_2, J_4, J_6, \dots$ are **numbers that encode how mass is distributed with depth.** A planet with a dense compact core produces a different set of $J_n$ than one of uniform density, even if their total masses are identical. Measure the $J_n$ precisely enough — by tracking, to within fractions of a millimetre per second, the Doppler shift of a spacecraft's radio signal as it whips past — and you can invert for the density profile.

{{image: Juno (spacecraft) | Juno, built not as a camera but as a gravity probe. From a close polar orbit it tracks the Doppler shift of its own radio signal to fractions of a millimetre per second, reading Jupiter's gravity harmonics — and through them the distribution of mass with depth inside a planet no instrument can enter.}}

This is what **Juno** was built to do. It arrived on 4 July 2016 into a highly eccentric polar orbit, diving to about **4,200 km above the cloud tops** at perijove — travelling at roughly **58 km/s**, the fastest any human object has ever moved relative to a planet — and then retreating far away for weeks before returning. The polar orbit and the close approach were both essential: to separate the higher-order $J_n$ you need to sample the field from many latitudes and very close in.

### What Juno found, and why it was a shock

The textbook picture of Jupiter, taught confidently for forty years, was **a distinct rocky-icy core of perhaps 10–20 Earth masses, sharply bounded, surrounded by an envelope of hydrogen and helium.** That structure follows naturally from core accretion: build a core, then dump gas on it. Juno's gravity data do not fit that picture.

What they support instead is a **dilute** — or "fuzzy" — **core**: a region extending out to perhaps **0.3–0.5 of Jupiter's radius** in which heavy elements are *gradually mixed* into the hydrogen, with no sharp boundary anywhere. The total heavy-element content is perhaps 7–25 Earth masses, but it is smeared across half the planet rather than concentrated at the middle.

Why does this matter enough to be, arguably, the single most important planetary-science result of the last decade? Because **it is a fossil of the formation process, and it says the formation was violent.** A compact core that quietly accreted gas should stay compact — hydrogen and rock do not readily mix at these conditions. To smear a core out across half a planet you need either (a) an extremely energetic event, such as a **head-on giant impact with a ~10 Earth-mass embryo** shortly after formation, which one prominent 2019 study proposed explicitly, or (b) a formation process in which heavy elements were never fully concentrated in the first place, because the core was still accreting while the envelope was arriving. Either way, the tidy sequential story — *core first, then gas* — is too clean. And note the methodological beauty: **we detected the aftermath of a collision 4.5 billion years ago by measuring the Doppler shift of a radio signal.**

Saturn, remarkably, tells a consistent story by a completely independent method. Because Saturn has rings, and the rings are a **seismometer.** Oscillations inside the planet perturb its gravity field, and those perturbations drive spiral density waves at resonant locations in the C ring — waves Cassini could see and measure. This field, **kronoseismology**, has yielded two beautiful results: Saturn also has a **diffuse core**, extending to roughly 60% of its radius and containing something like 17 Earth masses of heavy elements; and Saturn's true rotation period — long uncertain because its magnetic field is too symmetric to give a clock — is **10 h 33 m 38 s.** Saturn's interior was measured using its own rings as a detector — as elegant as anything in planetary science.

## The largest structure in the solar system

Recall the dynamo requirement from the terrestrial worlds: to generate a planetary magnetic field you need **an electrically conducting fluid, in motion, convecting, in a rotating body.** Earth barely qualifies — a molten iron outer core, 2,200 km thick, buried under 2,900 km of rock. Mars lost the fluid motion and lost the field, and then lost its atmosphere. Venus has the fluid but apparently not the convection.

Now consider Jupiter, which has an ocean of **liquid metallic hydrogen** occupying most of its volume, convecting vigorously because of its internal heat, in a body that spins in ten hours. The result is the most powerful planetary magnetic field in the solar system by an enormous margin: a **dipole moment roughly 20,000 times Earth's.** The equatorial surface field is about 4.2 gauss against Earth's ~0.3 — and Juno found that the field is far from a clean dipole, with local patches ranging over roughly an order of magnitude in strength, including a concentrated feature in the northern hemisphere the team named the **Great Blue Spot.** That irregularity is itself evidence: it suggests the dynamo operates in a shallower, more structured region than Earth's, possibly influenced by the dilute core.

### The scale of the magnetosphere

The magnetosphere — the cavity Jupiter's field carves out of the solar wind — stands off the wind at **50 to 100 Jupiter radii**, some 3.6 to 7 million kilometres upstream. Its tail streams downwind past the orbit of Saturn, more than 7 AU away, making it comfortably **the largest coherent structure in the solar system apart from the heliosphere itself.**

Here is the way to feel that number. If Jupiter's magnetosphere emitted visible light, it would appear from Earth at opposition as an object roughly **two and a half times the angular diameter of the full Moon.** The largest thing in our sky, invisible.

### Io, and a magnetosphere inflated from inside

Earth's magnetosphere is shaped by the solar wind pressing in from outside. Jupiter's is shaped mainly from **within**, and the agent is a moon.

**Io** is the most volcanically active body in the solar system, and it injects roughly **one tonne per second** of sulfur and oxygen into space. That material is ionised by solar UV, picked up by Jupiter's rapidly rotating field, and flung outward by centrifugal force into a doughnut of plasma — the **Io torus** — which then inflates the whole magnetosphere into a flattened, current-carrying **magnetodisc**.

{{image: Io (moon) | Io, the most volcanically active world in the solar system. Its eruptions feed roughly a tonne per second of sulfur and oxygen into Jupiter's magnetic field, inflating the largest structure in the solar system from the inside — and lighting a permanent auroral footprint that tracks the moon around the planet.}}

Take a moment with that. **The largest structure in the solar system is substantially the product of volcanoes on a moon 3,600 km across.** The coupling runs both ways, too: the electrical current flowing along field lines between Io and Jupiter — some millions of amperes — lands in Jupiter's polar atmosphere and lights a permanent **auroral footprint**, a bright spot that tracks Io's position around the planet. Ganymede and Europa have fainter ones. Jupiter's aurorae carry hundreds of times the power of Earth's, and unlike ours they are driven principally by the planet's own rotation rather than by the Sun.

### Radiation, and why Juno wears armour

Trapped in that field are electrons and ions accelerated to relativistic energies. The radiation environment around Jupiter is **lethal on timescales of hours** for unshielded electronics, and it is the single hardest engineering constraint on exploring the Jovian system — the reason Europa Clipper flies a looping trajectory that darts in and out rather than orbiting Europa directly. Juno's answer was brute force: a **titanium vault**, walls about a centimetre thick, roughly 200 kg, enclosing the spacecraft's brain. And its orbit was designed to thread the least-irradiated corridor available, diving over the poles and staying below the worst of the radiation belts at closest approach.

### Saturn's quiet anomaly

Saturn has a field too — weaker (about 0.2 gauss at the equator, roughly 1/20 of Jupiter's dipole moment relative to size), because its metallic hydrogen region is smaller and deeper, its lower mass reaching the transition pressure only at about half its radius.

But Saturn's field carries a genuine puzzle: it is **astonishingly axisymmetric.** Its magnetic axis is aligned with its rotation axis to within a fraction of a degree — less than 0.01° by Cassini's measurement. This is a real problem, because **Cowling's theorem** in dynamo theory says a perfectly axisymmetric field cannot be maintained by a dynamo. Something must be hiding the asymmetry: the leading explanation is a stably stratified, differentially rotating layer of helium-depleted hydrogen above the dynamo, acting as an axisymmetrising filter. And notice the practical consequence, which is how we got here in the first place: because the field has no tilt, it has **no wobble**, and therefore **no clock.** For every other planet we time the rotation of the deep interior by watching the magnetic field sweep past. For Saturn we could not — which is exactly why the ring-seismology measurement of 10 h 33 m 38 s mattered so much.

## Weather with no ground

Now to the thing everyone actually looks at: the stripes.

Jupiter's visible atmosphere is organised into alternating **belts** (dark, warmer, descending) and **zones** (bright, cooler, ascending), separated by **jet streams** running east and west at speeds up to about 150 m/s. Saturn's bands are the same phenomenon rendered in muted colours behind a high ammonia haze, with a monstrous equatorial jet reaching some **470 m/s — over 1,600 km/h**, more than three times Jupiter's. Two features deserve individual attention, and then one deep question.

### The Great Red Spot

An anticyclonic storm, larger than the Earth, that has been continuously observed since at least **1831** and quite possibly since Cassini's observations of a "permanent spot" in **1665.** Either way: a single storm system that has persisted for at least two centuries, and perhaps three and a half.

{{image: Great Red Spot | Jupiter's Great Red Spot, a single anticyclonic storm wider than the Earth, watched continuously since at least 1831. With no land to cross and no cold surface to drain into, a vortex trapped between two opposing jets simply keeps turning — though this one has shrunk by more than half in 140 years.}}

Why doesn't it die? On Earth a hurricane lasts days to weeks, because it runs over land or over cool water and loses its energy source. **There is no land on Jupiter.** There is no coastline to make landfall on, no cold surface to drain heat into, and no surface friction to dissipate the flow. A vortex on Jupiter, once formed, is confined between two opposing jets and simply keeps turning.

It is, however, **changing.** In 1879 it measured about 39,000 km across; by 2017 Juno measured roughly **16,350 km** — a **58% reduction in width in 140 years.** It is also becoming more circular and, oddly, taller. Whether this is a slow death or one phase of a long oscillation is genuinely unsettled. Juno also answered the question everyone had asked since Voyager: **how deep does it go?** By combining gravity measurements during overflights with microwave sounding, the answer is roughly **300–500 km** — far deeper than the visible clouds, but shallow compared to the jets that surround it. The Spot is a disc, not a column: about 100 times deeper than Earth's oceans, and yet a wafer on a body 70,000 km in radius.

### Saturn's hexagon

At Saturn's north pole there is a **hexagon.** Not a metaphorical one — a jet stream, roughly 30,000 km across (about two and a third Earth diameters), that traces six straight sides and six corners, and has done so continuously from Voyager's discovery in 1981 through Cassini's final orbits in 2017.

{{image: Saturn's hexagon | The hexagonal jet stream around Saturn's north pole — six straight sides, stable for at least four decades. Not an exotic mystery but a standing Rossby wave, the pattern a differentially rotating fluid selects in exactly this parameter range; laboratory tanks reproduce triangles, squares, and pentagons by tuning the spin.}}

This is the sort of thing that sounds like it demands an exotic explanation and, satisfyingly, does not get one. It is a **standing Rossby wave**: a jet stream that becomes unstable to a wave of a particular wavenumber and locks into a stationary polygonal pattern. The result has been **reproduced in the laboratory** — a rotating cylinder of fluid with a differentially spinning ring will spontaneously produce polygons, and by tuning the rotation rate you can select triangles, squares, pentagons, hexagons. Saturn's atmosphere happens to sit in the parameter range that selects six. This is a small lesson in how to react to strangeness. The correct response to a hexagon on a planet is not "how could nature draw a hexagon" but "what wavenumber does this flow select" — and then to go build the experiment.

### The deep question: how far down does the weather go?

For decades this was the central unresolved question of giant-planet meteorology, and it had two rival answers. Either the jets are a **shallow weather phenomenon** confined to the cloud layer, like Earth's; or they are the **surface expression of deep convective cylinders** running right through the planet parallel to the rotation axis (Taylor columns, a consequence of rapid rotation in a fluid).

Gravity settled it. Winds carry mass; mass makes gravity; **asymmetric winds make asymmetric gravity.** By measuring the north–south asymmetry of the gravity field, Juno at Jupiter and Cassini at Saturn each weighed their planet's winds directly. The answers:

- **Jupiter: the jets extend about 3,000 km deep** — roughly 4% of the radius, containing about 1% of the planet's mass.
- **Saturn: about 9,000 km** — roughly 15% of the radius.

Deep, then, compared to Earth's weather, but far from all the way down. And the punchline is the best part. Below those depths, both planets rotate essentially as **solid bodies** — and the transition depth in each case coincides with where hydrogen's **electrical conductivity** begins to rise sharply. Once the fluid conducts, the magnetic field resists shear across field lines and enforces uniform rotation. **The weather stops exactly where the fluid becomes a metal.** Meteorology hands off to magnetohydrodynamics at a boundary neither field would have guessed independently, and both spacecraft found it by weighing wind.

## The planets that are still warm

Now the fact that may be the most beautiful here, because it is measurable from Earth with a thermometer and it forces a conclusion about the deep interior. **Both Jupiter and Saturn radiate substantially more energy than they receive from the Sun.**

The bookkeeping is straightforward. Measure the sunlight falling on the planet, multiply by one minus the Bond albedo to get what is absorbed. Separately, measure the planet's infrared emission to get what is radiated. For a rock in equilibrium those numbers are equal. Here they are not:

| | Absorbed sunlight | Emitted radiation | Ratio | Internal power |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Jupiter** | 5.1 × 10¹⁷ W | 8.3 × 10¹⁷ W | **1.64** | 3.3 × 10¹⁷ W |
| **Saturn** | 1.0 × 10¹⁷ W | 2.0 × 10¹⁷ W | **1.9** | 0.9 × 10¹⁷ W |

Jupiter's internal heat flux is about **5.3 W/m²** at the cloud tops. Earth's geothermal flux, for comparison, is **0.087 W/m²** — Jupiter leaks about sixty times more heat per square metre than Earth does, over a surface area 120 times larger. This is why Jupiter's weather is driven from below. It is why the Galileo probe found winds strengthening with depth. And it demands an energy source, because whatever is powering this has been running for 4.5 billion years.

### Jupiter's answer: it is still falling

Jupiter's excess is explained straightforwardly by the **Kelvin–Helmholtz mechanism** — the same idea Kelvin and Helmholtz proposed (wrongly) for the Sun in the 19th century, which works beautifully here precisely because there is no fusion to complicate it. The planet is slowly contracting under its own gravity, and gravitational potential energy converts to heat, half of which is radiated away.

How fast must it shrink? Set the radiated power equal to the rate of release of gravitational energy:

$$L \approx \frac{3}{10}\frac{GM^2}{R^2}\frac{dR}{dt} \quad \Rightarrow \quad \frac{dR}{dt} \sim 1 \ \text{mm per year}$$

**About a millimetre a year.** A kilometre every million years; 700-odd kilometres per billion. Completely undetectable, and completely sufficient. And check the budget: the total gravitational energy reservoir is of order 10³⁶ J, which at the current luminosity would last roughly **100 billion years.** Jupiter has spent about 5% of its capital in 4.5 Gyr. There is no problem here at all.

### Saturn's answer: it is raining helium

Run the same calculation for Saturn and it fails. A 95-Earth-mass hydrogen sphere of Saturn's age, cooling and contracting, should produce roughly **half** the internal power we observe. Something else is contributing. The evidence for what comes from the atmospheric composition, and it is a genuinely lovely chain of inference.

The protosolar nebula had a helium mass fraction of about **Y ≈ 0.27.** These planets accreted directly from that gas, so their atmospheres ought to show the same value. Instead:

- **Jupiter's atmosphere**: Y ≈ **0.234** (measured *directly*, by the Galileo probe, in situ — one of the few numbers in this chapter obtained by physically sampling the gas)
- **Saturn's atmosphere**: Y ≈ **0.18–0.22**, distinctly lower still

Both are depleted relative to the gas they formed from. Saturn much more so. **Helium has left the observable atmosphere. It cannot have escaped upward — these planets lose essentially nothing to space. So it went down.**

The mechanism: at megabar pressures and the *right* temperatures, helium becomes **immiscible** in metallic hydrogen — the two stop mixing, exactly as oil and water separate. Helium condenses into droplets, and being denser, the droplets **rain** downward through tens of thousands of kilometres of liquid metallic hydrogen, releasing gravitational potential energy the whole way. This does two things at once, which is why the argument is so satisfying: it **removes helium from the upper atmosphere** (explaining the composition measurement) and it **releases heat** (explaining the energy excess). One mechanism, two independent observations.

And it explains the *difference* between the planets. Saturn is cooler inside than Jupiter, and it is the cooler conditions that push it deeper into the immiscibility region. So Saturn should be raining helium harder — which predicts both a **greater** helium depletion and a **larger** unexplained heat excess than Jupiter. Both are observed. The planet that is colder is hotter than it should be, for the reason that it is colder. There is even a possible third consequence: Saturn's helium rain may be what creates the stably-stratified layer that axisymmetrises its magnetic field. One phase-separation process, quietly explaining the heat budget, the composition, and possibly the dynamo.

## The instruments, and the deliberate deaths

A short section: the giant-planet story rests on three missions, and it is worth naming what each was for.

**Galileo (Jupiter, 1995–2003)** — the first orbiter of an outer planet, and the only probe ever dropped into a giant. It survived a crippled high-gain antenna that never unfurled, forcing the team to rewrite the onboard software to compress data and return the mission over a low-gain link at a tiny fraction of the intended rate. It nonetheless discovered the evidence for Europa's subsurface ocean, mapped Io's volcanism, and made the in-situ composition measurements we have been using throughout.

**Cassini–Huygens (Saturn, 2004–2017)** — arguably the most scientifically productive planetary mission ever flown. Thirteen years in orbit, the Huygens landing on Titan, the discovery of Enceladus's plumes, and finally the **Grand Finale**: 22 orbits threading the 2,000-km gap between Saturn's cloud tops and the inner edge of the rings, a trajectory nobody would have risked at any earlier point in the mission. Those orbits produced the ring-mass measurement and the gravity data that gave us Saturn's diffuse core and deep winds.

**Juno (Jupiter, 2016–present)** — deliberately *not* a camera mission. Its instrument list is dominated by a gravity science experiment, a magnetometer, and a microwave radiometer that sees hundreds of kilometres below the clouds. Nearly everything structural in this chapter — the dilute core, the jet depths, the field's irregularity — came from Juno. (JunoCam, the visible-light camera, was included largely for public outreach, and has produced some of the most beautiful images ever returned from space. There is a lesson in that too.)

### On deliberately destroying your own spacecraft

Both Galileo and Cassini ended the same way: **commanded to their deaths in the atmospheres of the planets they orbited.** Galileo on 21 September 2003, Cassini on 15 September 2017. Neither had failed. Both were destroyed on purpose, because they were running out of the propellant needed to control them, and an uncontrolled derelict might eventually strike **Europa** or **Enceladus** or **Titan** — worlds with liquid water beneath their surfaces — carrying terrestrial microbes that had survived the journey.

This is **planetary protection**, a legal obligation under the Outer Space Treaty and an ethical one under any reading. The principle is worth stating clearly, because it recurs throughout astrobiology:

> **We destroyed two of the finest instruments ever built rather than accept a small probability of contaminating a world where life might exist.**

That is what taking astrobiology seriously actually costs, in hardware, on a specific date. It is not a sentiment; it is an operational constraint that ends missions.

## Pulling the thread

Draw the argument together, because everything in this chapter descends from a single circumstance. Jupiter and Saturn formed **beyond the snow line**, where water ice condensed and the density of solids jumped by a factor of two to four. Their cores therefore grew fast enough to reach the runaway-accretion threshold **before the gas disk dissipated in a few million years**, and they captured the disk itself. Everything else follows:

- They are made of **hydrogen and helium**, so they have **no surface** — because hydrogen above its critical point admits no phase boundary.
- Hydrogen is **enormously compressible**, so Jupiter is twice Saturn's density from self-compression alone, and adding mass would eventually make them *smaller.*
- Compressed hydrogen becomes a **metal**, so they have vast conducting interiors, so they have the **strongest magnetic fields** and the largest magnetospheres in the solar system.
- Enough mass means enough gravitational energy that **contraction still powers them today**, so their weather is driven from below rather than above, so the storms are stable and the jets are deep.
- Being cold enough for helium to separate out of that metal, **Saturn rains**, which explains its excess heat, its missing helium, and possibly its impossibly symmetric field.
- And the same deep gravity we use to weigh their winds also reveals that their **cores are smeared, not compact** — telling us the formation was more violent and less orderly than the tidy story taught for forty years.

There is one more consequence, and it is the reason the giant-planet story continues rather than ending here. A planet this massive, forming this far out, **captures a disk of its own.** Jupiter and Saturn are not merely planets; they are systems, with dozens of moons arranged in a density sequence that echoes the solar system's own. And in that miniature disk, the tidal grip of an enormous parent supplies an energy source that has nothing whatever to do with sunlight. Which is how we end up looking for life in a place where the Sun is a bright star and the ocean is under ten kilometres of ice.

## Further reading

The science in this chapter is, unusually, almost entirely the product of three missions whose data and imagery are public: **Galileo**, **Cassini–Huygens**, and **Juno** (whose JunoCam raw images are released for anyone to process). Any modern planetary-science text's chapters on the giant planets will lay out the interior models and the gravity-harmonic method; for the discoveries themselves, the mission science pages are the primary source and are written to be read.

## Problems

*Constants you may need: $G = 6.674\times10^{-11}$, $k = 1.381\times10^{-23}$ J/K, $m_H = 1.673\times10^{-27}$ kg, $\sigma = 5.670\times10^{-8}$, 1 yr = $3.156 \times 10^{7}$ s. Jupiter: $M = 1.898\times10^{27}$ kg, $R_{vol} = 69{,}911$ km, $R_{eq} = 71{,}492$ km. Saturn: $M = 5.683\times10^{26}$ kg, $R_{vol} = 58{,}232$ km, $R_{eq} = 60{,}268$ km. Earth: $M = 5.972\times10^{24}$ kg, $R = 6{,}371$ km. Problem 2 (the density comparison) teaches self-compression; problem 5 (the helium argument) is the conceptual core; problem 7 (weighing the winds) is the finest piece of measurement reasoning here; problem 9 has no settled answer and is meant to be argued about.*

**1 — The correct picture of the solar system.** **(a)** How many Earths would fit inside Jupiter by volume? Inside Saturn? **(b)** Using the mass table, what percentage of all planetary mass is Jupiter alone? Jupiter and Saturn together? Everything else? **(c)** In one sentence, state the mental picture of the solar system these numbers should replace, and what should replace it.

**2 — Two planets, one recipe.** Jupiter and Saturn have essentially the same bulk composition — roughly solar hydrogen and helium with a modest heavy-element enrichment. **(a)** Compute the mean density of each. **(b)** Jupiter is 3.34 times Saturn's mass but only 1.20 times its radius. What does that tell you about how radius responds to mass in this regime? **(c)** Explain the factor-of-two density difference **without** invoking any difference in composition. **(d)** Now extend the logic: predict, qualitatively, what happens to radius if you keep adding mass past Jupiter. What observational consequence does this have for the exoplanet catalogue?

**3 — Why Jupiter is not a star.** **(a)** Express Jupiter's mass as a fraction of the Sun's. **(b)** By what factor would Jupiter's mass need to increase to burn deuterium? To burn hydrogen? **(c)** Jupiter's central temperature is about 20,000 K. Hydrogen fusion requires of order $10^{7}$ K. By what factor does it fall short? **(d)** Criticise the phrase "failed star." What is actually wrong with it — not just quantitatively, but as a claim about *history*?

**4 — The skin.** **(a)** Compute the atmospheric scale height for Jupiter ($g = 24.79$ m/s², $T = 165$ K, $\mu = 2.22$) and Saturn ($g = 10.44$, $T = 134$ K, $\mu = 2.07$). **(b)** Five scale heights spans a pressure range of a factor of $e^5 \approx 150$. Express $5H$ as a percentage of each planet's equatorial radius. **(c)** Saturn's $H$ is twice Jupiter's despite Saturn being colder. Why? **(d)** What does (b) tell you about the relationship between what we photograph and what we are trying to understand?

**5 — The helium argument (the conceptual core).** You are given three facts: (i) the protosolar nebula had helium mass fraction $Y \approx 0.27$; (ii) Jupiter's atmosphere measures $Y \approx 0.234$ and Saturn's $Y \approx 0.18$–$0.22$; (iii) Saturn radiates roughly twice as much energy as it absorbs, about double what contraction alone can supply. **(a)** Why can't the missing helium have escaped to space? **(b)** Construct the argument that links (ii) and (iii) to a single physical mechanism. **(c)** The mechanism requires helium to be *immiscible* in metallic hydrogen — which happens more readily at lower temperature. Saturn's interior is cooler than Jupiter's. What does that predict about the *relative* helium depletion and heat excess of the two planets? Is it observed? **(d)** Why is this argument stronger than one that explained only the heat, or only the composition?

**6 — Weighing the fall.** Jupiter's internal power is $3.3\times10^{17}$ W. Model it as a uniform sphere, for which the gravitational binding energy is $U \approx \tfrac{3}{10}GM^2/R$. **(a)** How fast must Jupiter's radius be shrinking, in mm/yr, to supply this power? **(b)** How long would the full reservoir last at the present rate? **(c)** What fraction of the reservoir has been spent in 4.5 Gyr? **(d)** Real planets are centrally concentrated, so the true coefficient is larger than 3/10. Does that make the contraction faster or slower, and does it strengthen or weaken the explanation?

**7 — Weighing the winds (the best measurement here).** Both Juno and Cassini determined how deep their planets' jet streams penetrate. Neither carried an instrument that measures wind at depth. **(a)** Explain the physical chain that lets a *gravity* measurement reveal a *wind* depth. Why specifically the north–south **asymmetry** of the field? **(b)** The answers were ~3,000 km (Jupiter) and ~9,000 km (Saturn). Express each as a percentage of the planet's radius. **(c)** Below those depths both planets rotate as solid bodies. What sets that boundary, and why is the coincidence with hydrogen's electrical conductivity not a coincidence? **(d)** Why was this question unanswerable before Juno and Cassini's Grand Finale, given that we had orbited both planets for years?

**8 — The shape of a spinning fluid.** **(a)** Compute $q = \omega^2 R_{eq}/g_{eq}$, the ratio of centrifugal to gravitational acceleration at the equator, for Jupiter (period 9 h 55 m 30 s) and Saturn (10 h 33 m 38 s). **(b)** Saturn's observed oblateness (0.098) exceeds Jupiter's (0.065) even though Saturn spins *more slowly*. Resolve the apparent paradox. **(c)** For a uniform-density fluid body the flattening is approximately $f \approx \tfrac{5}{4}q$. Compute this for both and compare to the observed values. The prediction overshoots. Why — and what does the size of the overshoot tell you? **(d)** Saturn's rotation period was genuinely uncertain for decades. Why is that surprising, and how was it eventually pinned down?

**9 — Open problem: the core that isn't there.** Juno's gravity data indicate Jupiter has a **dilute** core — heavy elements smeared out to perhaps half the planet's radius — rather than the compact 10–20 Earth-mass core that core-accretion theory predicts and that textbooks asserted for decades. **(a)** Why is a *smeared* core hard to reconcile with the standard "build a core, then accrete gas" sequence? **(b)** Sketch the two leading explanations. **(c)** What independent evidence, from a different planet and a completely different technique, points the same way? **(d)** Design the observation or calculation that would most cleanly discriminate between the two explanations in (b). Be specific about what you would measure and what each hypothesis predicts.

## Worked answers

**1 — The correct picture of the solar system.** **(a)** $(R_J/R_E)^3 = (69{,}911/6{,}371)^3 = \mathbf{1{,}321}$ Earths by volume inside Jupiter; $(58{,}232/6{,}371)^3 = \mathbf{764}$ inside Saturn. **(b)** Total planetary mass = 446.6 $M_\oplus$. Jupiter alone = **71.2%.** Jupiter + Saturn = **92.5%.** Everything else — both ice giants, all four terrestrial planets, every moon, asteroid and comet — = **7.5%.** **(c)** Replace *"the Sun and its eight planets, arranged in a row"* with **"the Sun, then Jupiter, then a long tail of debris — and we live in the debris."** The eight-beads picture is not merely simplified, it is misleading about mass by a factor of thirty; it invites you to think of Earth as a typical member of a family when it is a 0.2% contaminant.

**2 — Two planets, one recipe.** **(a)** $\rho_J = M/(\tfrac{4}{3}\pi R^3) = \mathbf{1{,}326}$ kg/m³; $\rho_S = \mathbf{687}$ kg/m³ (less than water, 1,000 kg/m³). **(b)** Mass ratio 3.34, radius ratio only 1.20. If density were constant, tripling the mass would increase radius by $3.34^{1/3} = 1.50$. It increases by only 1.20. **Radius is a very weak function of mass** here: the added material is largely absorbed by compressing what is already there. **(c)** **Self-compression.** Both planets are made of hydrogen and helium, which are extraordinarily compressible. Jupiter's greater mass means far higher interior pressures, so the *same* material is squeezed into a smaller volume per kilogram. The factor-of-1.93 density difference is a statement about gravity, not chemistry. (For a body made of compressible material, **mean density is a measure of mass, not of composition** — a lesson that matters enormously when interpreting exoplanet densities.) **(d)** Adding mass increases the radius more and more slowly, the curve turns over at roughly **3–4 $M_J$**, and beyond that radius *decreases* with mass as electron degeneracy pressure takes over ($R \propto M^{-1/3}$). Observationally: **objects from about 0.5 to 80 Jupiter masses are all roughly Jupiter-sized.** A transit measurement, which gives radius, therefore constrains the mass of a giant planet hardly at all — you need a radial-velocity or transit-timing mass. Radius and mass are nearly independent variables in this regime, which is precisely why the exoplanet field needs both techniques.

**3 — Why Jupiter is not a star.** **(a)** $M_J/M_\odot = 9.54\times10^{-4}$, i.e. about **1/1048.** **(b)** Deuterium burning at ~13 $M_J$ → a factor of **13.** Hydrogen burning at ~0.075–0.080 $M_\odot \approx 80\,M_J$ → a factor of about **80.** **(c)** $10^7/2\times10^4 = $ **a factor of ~500** in temperature. And since reaction rates depend on temperature to a very high power, the fusion rate falls short by an astronomical margin, not a marginal one. **(d)** Two objections. **Quantitatively**, "failed" implies "nearly succeeded," and 80× is not nearly. But the deeper objection is **historical**: stars form top-down, by the gravitational collapse and fragmentation of a molecular cloud. Jupiter formed bottom-up, by core accretion — ice and rock coagulating into a solid core beyond the snow line, which then captured gas. **Jupiter was never on a trajectory toward being a star.** It is not an underachieving star; it is a completely successful planet that happens to share a composition with one. The phrase imports a false teleology.

**4 — The skin.** **(a)** $H = kT/(\mu m_H g)$. Jupiter: $H = (1.381\times10^{-23} \times 165)/(2.22 \times 1.673\times10^{-27} \times 24.79) = \mathbf{24.7}$ km. Saturn: $\mathbf{51.2}$ km. **(b)** $5H$: Jupiter 124 km = **0.17%** of $R_{eq}$; Saturn 256 km = **0.43%.** **(c)** $H \propto T/g$. Saturn's temperature is lower by a factor of 0.81, but its gravity is lower by a factor of 0.42 — and the gravity term wins decisively. **Weak gravity puffs an atmosphere out faster than cold shrinks it.** **(d)** Every photograph ever taken of Jupiter shows a layer two parts in a thousand deep. The clouds are a **skin on an object we cannot see**, and essentially everything structurally important — the metallic hydrogen, the dynamo, the dilute core, the heat source — lies beneath it and must be inferred indirectly, from gravity, magnetism, and thermal emission. This is why Juno is a gravity mission with a camera bolted on for the public, rather than a camera mission.

**5 — The helium argument.** **(a)** Escape requires the thermal velocity of the gas to approach the escape velocity. Jupiter's escape velocity is **59.5 km/s** and its exosphere is cold; even hydrogen, the lightest gas there is, is retained essentially completely. **A planet that cannot lose hydrogen certainly cannot selectively lose helium.** The helium did not go up, so it went down. **(b)** The chain: (1) both planets accreted directly from nebular gas with $Y \approx 0.27$, so their atmospheres should show that value; (2) both show less, Saturn much less, so helium has been removed from the observable envelope; (3) it cannot have escaped, so it must have sunk; (4) sinking requires helium to separate as a distinct phase — **immiscibility in metallic hydrogen at megabar pressures**, forming droplets denser than the surrounding fluid; (5) those droplets fall through tens of thousands of kilometres, converting gravitational potential energy to heat; (6) **one mechanism therefore produces both the depleted atmosphere and the excess luminosity.** **(c)** Immiscibility is favoured at *lower* temperature. Saturn's interior is cooler, so it sits further inside the immiscibility region and should be raining harder — predicting **both** greater helium depletion **and** a larger unexplained heat excess relative to Jupiter. **Both are observed.** The elegance is in the inversion: the colder planet is hotter than it ought to be, *because* it is colder. **(d)** Because it makes a **joint prediction across two independent observables** measured by different instruments (in-situ mass spectrometry for composition; infrared photometry and bolometry for the energy budget), plus a **differential prediction** between two planets that could have come out the other way. An explanation tuned to one observation is a parameter; an explanation that constrains three is a theory. It is exactly the standard you would demand of a phosphine or DMS biosignature claim.

**6 — Weighing the fall.** **(a)** $L = \tfrac{3}{10}\dfrac{GM^2}{R^2}\dfrac{dR}{dt}$. With $L = 3.3\times10^{17}$ W: $\dfrac{dR}{dt} = \dfrac{3.3\times10^{17}}{0.3 \times (6.674\times10^{-11})(1.898\times10^{27})^2/(6.99\times10^{7})^2} = 2.3\times10^{-11}$ m/s $= \mathbf{0.7\ mm/yr}$ — about **700 km per billion years**, or 1 km every million years. **(b)** $U = \tfrac{3}{10}GM^2/R = 1.03\times10^{36}$ J; $U/L = 3.1\times10^{18}$ s $= \mathbf{\sim100\ Gyr}$. **(c)** Radiated over 4.5 Gyr $\approx 4.6\times10^{34}$ J, i.e. about **4.5%** of the reservoir. There is no energy crisis whatsoever — Jupiter has barely begun to spend. **(d)** A larger coefficient means *more* energy released per metre of contraction, so the required $dR/dt$ is **slower** (roughly 0.35 mm/yr for $k=0.6$) and the reservoir is **larger** (~200 Gyr). It **strengthens** the explanation: the mechanism works comfortably under any plausible assumption, and the conclusion is robust to a factor-of-two ignorance about the interior. An explanation that survives your uncertainty is worth more than one that requires you to resolve it.

**7 — Weighing the winds.** **(a)** The chain: **winds are moving mass** → moving mass is *displaced* mass → displaced mass perturbs the external gravity field. A wind pattern extending to depth $d$ carries more mass, and perturbs gravity more, than the same surface pattern confined to the clouds. So the *magnitude* of the gravity perturbation encodes the *depth* of the flow. Why the **asymmetry** specifically: a spherically symmetric, uniformly rotating planet produces only *even* gravity harmonics ($J_2, J_4, J_6$). The **odd** harmonics ($J_3, J_5, J_7, J_9$) vanish identically for a north–south symmetric body. Jupiter's jets are *not* symmetric about the equator — so the odd harmonics are essentially **pure wind signal**, uncontaminated by the far larger static structure. Juno measured $J_3$ and $J_5$ at nonzero values, and the depth followed. This is a beautiful piece of experimental design: find the observable to which your signal contributes and your background does not. **(b)** Jupiter: 3,000/71,492 = **4.2%** of the radius. Saturn: 9,000/60,268 = **14.9%.** Jupiter's jets hold roughly 1% of its mass — a thin shell by mass, but 3,000 km is still deeper than Earth's mantle. **(c)** The boundary is set by the onset of significant **electrical conductivity** in hydrogen as it is compressed. Once the fluid conducts, differential rotation would shear the magnetic field, and the resulting Lorentz forces (and ohmic dissipation) act as a brake, enforcing near-uniform rotation. This is **not** a coincidence but a causal relation: the winds stop where the magnetic field starts being able to stop them. The transition is deeper in Saturn as a fraction of radius, because Saturn's lower mass means the required pressure is reached further in — **which is exactly why Saturn's jets run three times deeper.** The same physics predicts both numbers. **(d)** Because gravity science of this precision requires **very close, very high-latitude passes** at many longitudes and phases. Galileo and Cassini spent their prime missions in near-equatorial, moon-focused tours far from their planets — perfect for satellite science, useless for measuring high-order gravity harmonics. Juno was purpose-built with a polar, low-perijove orbit; Cassini only got the equivalent data in the **Grand Finale**, on 22 orbits it would never have flown while the spacecraft was still needed for anything else. **The measurement required a trajectory that was too risky to fly until the mission was ending.**

**8 — The shape of a spinning fluid.** **(a)** $\omega = 2\pi/T$. Jupiter: $T = 35{,}730$ s, $\omega = 1.759\times10^{-4}$ s⁻¹, $g_{eq} = GM/R^2 = 24.79$ m/s², $q = \omega^2 R/g = \mathbf{0.089}$. Saturn: $T = 38{,}018$ s, $\omega = 1.653\times10^{-4}$ s⁻¹, $g_{eq} = 10.44$ m/s², $q = \mathbf{0.158}$. **(b)** Oblateness is set not by rotation rate alone but by the **ratio of centrifugal to gravitational acceleration.** Saturn spins 6% slower, but its surface gravity is only **42%** of Jupiter's. The weak gravity dominates, and $q$ is **1.8× larger** for Saturn. Same spin, weaker grip, bigger bulge. **(c)** $\tfrac{5}{4}q$: Jupiter **0.111** (observed 0.065); Saturn **0.198** (observed 0.098). The prediction **overshoots by 1.7× and 2.0×** respectively. The reason is the assumption: $f \approx \tfrac{5}{4}q$ holds for a body of **uniform density.** Real giant planets are strongly **centrally concentrated** — mass is piled at the middle, where the centrifugal effect is negligible, so less of the planet participates in the bulging. **The size of the overshoot is a direct measure of central concentration.** This is precisely the logic that turns oblateness and $J_2$ into a probe of interior structure — the crude version of what Juno did with high precision. **(d)** Surprising because rotation is the most basic property of a planet and we have watched Saturn for 400 years. But the *clouds* rotate differentially (that is what a jet stream is), so cloud tracking gives you weather, not the interior. The standard trick — timing the periodic wobble of the tilted magnetic field — **fails at Saturn because the field has essentially no tilt** (<0.01°), leaving no clock. The answer, **10 h 33 m 38 s**, came from **kronoseismology**: internal oscillations perturb Saturn's gravity field, driving spiral density waves at resonant radii in the C ring, which Cassini observed and inverted. **Saturn's rotation was measured using its rings as a seismometer.**

**9 — Open problem: the core that isn't there.** **(a)** Core accretion is a **sequential** story: solids coagulate into a ~10 $M_\oplus$ core, which then triggers runaway capture of gas. That predicts a **compositional discontinuity** — a distinct core with an envelope on top. Moreover, hydrogen and heavy elements do not readily mix at these pressures, and a compact core, once formed, has no obvious way to disperse itself across half a planet. **The smearing has to be explained, and the standard sequence provides no mechanism for it.** **(b)** Two families: **a giant impact** — a body of order 10 $M_\oplus$ strikes Jupiter head-on shortly after formation, delivering enough energy to disrupt and mix the core into the overlying envelope, which then does not re-settle because the mixed state is stable against convection; or **formation was never sequential** — the core continued accreting solids *while* the envelope was arriving, so heavy elements were delivered into the envelope throughout rather than concentrated first, a dilute composition gradient that is primordial rather than the result of an event (a related variant is core erosion by the hot envelope over Gyr timescales). **(c)** **Saturn**, by **kronoseismology** — a completely independent planet, measured by a completely independent technique (ring density waves rather than spacecraft Doppler tracking). It also shows a **diffuse core**, extending to roughly 60% of its radius. Two planets, two methods, one conclusion: this is not an artefact of Juno's data reduction. That independent confirmation is what moved the dilute core from "surprising result" to "thing we now believe." **(d)** Good answers should be **discriminating, not merely confirmatory.** The strongest lines: **population statistics from exoplanets** — a giant impact is a *stochastic* event, primordial gradual enrichment is *generic*, so measure the interior structure of many giant planets; if dilute cores are near-universal, impacts are the wrong explanation (you cannot have a rare event happen every time), while if they are found in perhaps half, stochasticity is supported (arguably the cleanest test, and a decade or more away). **The shape of the composition gradient** — an impact-mixed core and a gradually-accreted one should produce different *profiles* of heavy-element fraction with radius (an impact should leave a more abruptly truncated, more thoroughly homogenised inner region); this demands higher-order gravity harmonics than we currently have, plus better equations of state. **Seismology of Jupiter** — oscillation modes probe the sound-speed profile directly and would resolve the gradient far better than gravity can, but Jupiter has no rings to act as a detector, so this requires a dedicated Doppler-imaging campaign or a very long-lived orbiter. **Laboratory equations of state** — much of the inversion's uncertainty is not in the data but in how hydrogen–helium–rock mixtures behave at 10–40 Mbar, so better shock-compression data would tighten both models and might dissolve part of the disagreement. The best answers will also say what would **not** settle it: another gravity mission at Jupiter alone, however precise, cannot distinguish an event that happened once from a process that happens always. This is a question about a population, and it needs a population.$astroGas_master$,
    true,
    true
  )
