with q as (
  select $astroGas_quiz$[{"id": "q1", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Saturn's mean density (687 kg/m3) is less than water's, while Jupiter's (1,326 kg/m3) is nearly twice Saturn's. Both are made of essentially the same hydrogen-helium mixture in solar proportions. What actually explains the density difference?", "options": ["Saturn contains far more hydrogen and less rock than Jupiter", "SELF-COMPRESSION: Jupiter is 3.3x more massive, so its interior squeezes itself far harder, and hydrogen is enormously compressible \u2014 the same material is packed into a smaller volume per kilogram. The factor-of-two density difference is a statement about gravity, not chemistry. The same logic means adding mass past ~3-4 Jupiter masses would make the planet SMALLER, as electron degeneracy takes over (R \u221d M^\u22121/3)", "Saturn's rings lower its measured bulk density", "Jupiter is hotter inside, and hot gas is denser than cold gas"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Both planets are the same stuff, so composition cannot be the answer. A pile of hydrogen crushes itself: the weight of overlying material compresses the interior, and hydrogen is extraordinarily compressible. Jupiter is 3.34x Saturn's mass, so its interior pressures are far higher and the same material is squeezed into a smaller volume per kilogram \u2014 giving it nearly twice the density with no difference in recipe. This is why mean density in this regime measures MASS, not composition. Pushed to its limit the same physics is startling: past about 3-4 Jupiter masses, adding mass makes a cold hydrogen object shrink, because it is held up by electron degeneracy pressure (R \u221d M^\u22121/3). That is why brown dwarfs up to 80 Jupiter masses are all roughly Jupiter-sized, and why a transit radius tells you almost nothing about a giant planet's mass."}, {"id": "q2", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "The Galileo probe made a flawless 58-minute descent into Jupiter in 1995 and reported the atmosphere was nearly DRY \u2014 oxygen depleted several-fold below solar, contradicting formation theory. What was actually going on?", "options": ["Jupiter genuinely formed without water, overturning the snow-line theory", "The probe's water detector had failed during the violent entry", "The probe fell by bad luck into a 5-micron HOT SPOT \u2014 one of a few unusually dry, cloud-free downdrafts covering a tiny fraction of the planet. A technically perfect instrument sampled Jupiter's equivalent of the Sahara and reported the whole planet was dry. A perfect instrument in the wrong place is not data; it is an anecdote with error bars", "Jupiter's water had sunk below the probe's final 22-bar depth and was simply out of reach everywhere"], "answerIndex": 2, "explanation": "The measurement was real and the hardware worked; the problem was SAMPLING. The probe descended, entirely by chance, into a 5-micron hot spot \u2014 one of a small number of unusually dry, cloud-free downdrafts that cover only a tiny fraction of Jupiter's surface at any time. It parachuted into the planet's Sahara and reported that Jupiter had no water, much as a single probe into a terrestrial desert might report that Earth has no oceans. For years this looked like a genuine crisis for formation theory before the hot-spot explanation resolved it. The lesson recurs across planetary science and astrobiology (the Viking Labeled Release result on Mars is a cousin): a single flawless measurement can mislead an entire field when the location it sampled is unrepresentative. Later microwave mapping by Juno found Jupiter's water abundance is in fact enhanced, as theory required."}, {"id": "q3", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Juno's precise gravity measurements overturned the 40-year textbook picture of Jupiter's interior. What did they find, and why does it matter?", "options": ["A compact 10-20 Earth-mass rocky core exactly as predicted, confirming core accretion", "A DILUTE or 'fuzzy' core \u2014 heavy elements gradually smeared through the inner ~half of the planet with no sharp boundary, rather than a compact core. Because a quietly-accreted core should stay compact, the smearing implies the formation was violent (a giant impact) or never sequential \u2014 a 4.5-billion-year-old fossil read from the Doppler shift of a radio signal, and independently confirmed at Saturn by ring seismology", "That Jupiter has no core at all and is pure hydrogen and helium throughout", "A molten iron core like Earth's, generating the magnetic field"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The external gravity field of a spinning planet is a series in coefficients J2, J4, J6, ... that encode how mass is distributed with depth. By tracking Juno's radio Doppler shift to fractions of a millimetre per second from a close polar orbit, the team inverted those coefficients for Jupiter's density profile \u2014 and found not the sharply bounded 10-20 Earth-mass core that core accretion predicts and textbooks asserted, but a DILUTE core: heavy elements gradually mixed out to perhaps 0.3-0.5 of the radius with no boundary anywhere. This is a fossil of formation, and it says formation was not tidy: a compact core does not spontaneously smear (hydrogen and rock do not readily mix), so you need either an energetic event like a head-on giant impact with a ~10 Earth-mass body, or a formation in which heavy elements were never concentrated in the first place. Saturn tells the same story by an entirely independent method \u2014 kronoseismology, its rings acting as a seismometer \u2014 which is what turned a surprising result into a believed one."}, {"id": "q4", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Saturn radiates about 1.9 times as much energy as it absorbs from the Sun \u2014 roughly double what slow gravitational contraction alone can supply. Its atmosphere is also depleted in helium relative to the gas it formed from. What single mechanism explains BOTH facts?", "options": ["Residual radioactive decay in Saturn's core heats it and destroys helium", "HELIUM RAIN: at Saturn's interior pressures and (crucially) cooler temperatures, helium becomes immiscible in metallic hydrogen, condenses into dense droplets, and rains down through tens of thousands of km \u2014 removing helium from the upper atmosphere AND releasing gravitational heat on the way down. One mechanism, two observations; and because Saturn is cooler than Jupiter it rains harder, so it is both more helium-depleted and more heat-excessive", "Saturn absorbs extra energy from Jupiter's magnetosphere", "Ongoing hydrogen fusion in Saturn's core, at a low rate"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Plain Kelvin-Helmholtz contraction accounts for Jupiter's heat excess but falls a factor of two short for Saturn, and separately Saturn's atmosphere shows a helium mass fraction (Y ~ 0.18-0.22) well below the protosolar value (~0.27) it accreted from. Helium cannot have escaped \u2014 a planet that retains even hydrogen cannot selectively lose helium \u2014 so it went DOWN. At megabar pressures and the right (lower) temperatures helium becomes immiscible in metallic hydrogen, exactly as oil separates from water; it condenses into droplets denser than the surrounding fluid, which rain downward, converting gravitational potential energy to heat the whole way. That simultaneously removes helium from the observable envelope (explaining the composition) and releases energy (explaining the excess luminosity). The clincher is the differential prediction: immiscibility is favoured at lower temperature, Saturn's interior is cooler than Jupiter's, so Saturn should rain harder and show BOTH greater helium depletion and a larger heat excess \u2014 and both are observed. The colder planet is hotter than it should be, precisely because it is colder."}, {"id": "q5", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Neither Juno nor Cassini carried an instrument that measures wind at depth, yet each determined how deep its planet's jet streams penetrate (Jupiter ~3,000 km, Saturn ~9,000 km). How, and what sets the depth where the jets stop?", "options": ["They dropped probes that measured wind directly all the way down", "They measured the north-south ASYMMETRY of the gravity field: winds are moving mass, and a north-south asymmetric wind pattern produces nonzero ODD gravity harmonics (J3, J5, ...) that vanish for a symmetric static body \u2014 so the odd harmonics are almost pure wind signal, and their magnitude encodes the flow's depth. The jets stop where compressed hydrogen becomes electrically conducting, so the magnetic field brakes differential rotation \u2014 weather hands off to magnetohydrodynamics", "They timed how long storms took to circle each planet", "They inferred wind depth from the planets' oblateness alone"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Winds carry mass, displaced mass perturbs the external gravity field, and a deeper flow carries more mass and perturbs gravity more \u2014 so the magnitude of the gravity anomaly encodes the depth of the wind. The elegant part is isolating the signal: a north-south symmetric, uniformly rotating planet produces only EVEN harmonics (J2, J4, J6), so the ODD harmonics (J3, J5, J7) vanish identically for the static background. Jupiter's jets are not symmetric about the equator, so the odd harmonics Juno measured are essentially pure wind signal, uncontaminated by the vastly larger static structure \u2014 a textbook case of finding the observable your signal contributes to and your background does not. The depths came out ~3,000 km (Jupiter, ~4% of radius) and ~9,000 km (Saturn, ~15%). Below those depths both planets rotate as solid bodies, and the transition coincides with where hydrogen's electrical conductivity rises sharply: once the fluid conducts, shearing the magnetic field costs energy, and the resulting forces enforce near-uniform rotation. The weather stops exactly where the fluid becomes a metal \u2014 and Saturn's jets run deeper because its lower mass reaches that pressure further in."}, {"id": "q6", "type": "open", "prompt": "Jupiter and Saturn together are 92.5% of all planetary mass in the solar system, and Jupiter alone is 2.47 times every other planet combined. (a) State the corrected mental picture these numbers demand. (b) Jupiter is made of the same hydrogen and helium as the Sun \u2014 explain precisely why calling it a 'failed star' is wrong, both quantitatively and as a claim about history.", "rubric": "THE CORRECTED PICTURE (a): The schoolroom image of the Sun plus eight planets 'in a row like beads' is misleading about mass by a factor of ~30. The correct picture is: the Sun, then Jupiter, then a long tail of debris \u2014 and we live in the debris. To first approximation the solar system is a star and two balls of hydrogen (Jupiter + Saturn = 92.5% of planetary mass); every other planet, moon, asteroid, comet, and the entire Kuiper belt is a ~7.5% garnish. Earth is a ~0.2% contaminant, not a typical family member. WHY 'FAILED STAR' IS WRONG (b): QUANTITATIVELY \u2014 'failed' implies 'nearly succeeded,' but Jupiter falls short by large factors, not marginally. It is 1/1048 of a solar mass; deuterium fusion (the brown-dwarf threshold, the easiest reaction) needs ~13 Jupiter masses, so Jupiter is short by ~13x; true hydrogen fusion needs ~0.075-0.080 solar masses ~ 80 Jupiter masses, short by ~80x; and its central temperature (~20,000 K) is a factor of ~500 below the ~10^7 K fusion requires, which given the steep temperature dependence of reaction rates means it misses by an astronomical margin. HISTORICALLY (the deeper objection) \u2014 stars form top-down, by gravitational collapse and fragmentation of a molecular cloud; Jupiter formed bottom-up by core accretion, an ice-and-rock core growing beyond the snow line to ~10 Earth masses and then capturing disk gas in runaway accretion. Jupiter was never on a trajectory toward becoming a star; it is not an underachieving star but a completely successful planet that happens to share a composition with one. The phrase imports a false teleology. A strong answer notes the two objects share composition and almost nothing else about their history."}, {"id": "q7", "type": "open", "prompt": "The chapter argues that almost every strange fact about Jupiter and Saturn descends from a single circumstance: they are made almost entirely of hydrogen, in quantities large enough that it stops behaving like itself. Trace that chain \u2014 starting from the snow line, through 'no surface,' to the magnetic fields, the internal heat, and the deep/stable weather \u2014 showing how each property follows from the last.", "rubric": "A strong answer reconstructs the causal chain, not just a list. START \u2014 THE SNOW LINE: beyond ~2.7 AU water ice condenses, the surface density of solids jumps 2-4x, so cores reach the critical ~10 Earth masses fast enough to trigger runaway gas accretion BEFORE the gas disk dissipates in a few Myr; Jupiter and Saturn captured the disk itself, so they are made of ~solar hydrogen and helium. NO SURFACE: hydrogen above its critical point admits no gas-liquid phase boundary, so there is no ground \u2014 just a continuous thickening from gas to liquid to metal, and 'the radius of Jupiter' is a 1-bar convention, with the visible clouds a skin ~0.4% of the radius deep. COMPRESSIBILITY: hydrogen is enormously compressible, so Jupiter is ~2x Saturn's density by self-compression alone, and adding mass past ~3-4 Jupiter masses would make the planet smaller (electron degeneracy, R \u221d M^\u22121/3) \u2014 brown dwarfs are Jupiter-sized. METALLIC HYDROGEN -> FIELDS: compressed to ~1-2 Mbar hydrogen ionises into liquid metallic hydrogen, an excellent conductor filling most of the volume; convecting vigorously in a body spinning in ~10 hours, it drives the strongest planetary magnetic field in the solar system (dipole ~20,000x Earth's) and the largest magnetosphere (standing off at 50-100 Jupiter radii, tail past Saturn's orbit) \u2014 inflated further from within by ~1 tonne/s of ionised material from Io's volcanism. INTERNAL HEAT -> WEATHER FROM BELOW: enough mass means enough gravitational energy that Kelvin-Helmholtz contraction (~1 mm/yr) still powers the planet today \u2014 both radiate more than they absorb \u2014 so weather is driven from below rather than by sunlight, storms have no land or cold surface to dissipate on (the Great Red Spot has lasted centuries), and the jets run thousands of km deep, stopping only where hydrogen becomes a metal and the magnetic field enforces solid-body rotation. SATURN'S REFINEMENT: cooler, so helium becomes immiscible and rains out, explaining its extra heat, its missing helium, and possibly its impossibly axisymmetric field. CLOSING: the same deep gravity that weighs the winds also shows the cores are smeared, not compact \u2014 formation was more violent than the tidy story. The very best answers close on the forward hook: a planet this massive captures a disk of its own, becoming a system of moons with a tidal energy source independent of the Sun \u2014 which is why we look for life under the ice of a moon where the Sun is only a bright star."}]$astroGas_quiz$::jsonb as questions
)
insert into quizzes (node_id, level, questions, generated)
select 'astronomy-and-space/solar-system/giant-planets/gas-giants', lvl, q.questions, true
from q, (values ('easy'), ('advanced'), ('expert')) as l(lvl)
on conflict (node_id, level) do update
  set questions = excluded.questions, generated = excluded.generated;
